From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Toon Verwaest <verwaest@chromium.org>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 16:30:49 +0200
Subject: [parser] Parse bracketed property keys and template spans as
 expressions

Bracketed property keys (`obj[expr]` and `obj?.[expr]`) and template
literal interpolations (`` `${expr}` ``) are unconditionally
`Expression`s in the ECMAScript grammar and can never be destructuring
patterns or arrow parameter lists.

Previously, `ParseLeftHandSideContinuation`,
`DoParseMemberExpressionContinuation`, and `ParseTemplateLiteral` called
`ParseExpressionCoverGrammar()` directly in the enclosing
`ExpressionScope`. When enclosed in a `VariableDeclarationParsingScope`
(e.g. `let [a, b[a]] = []` or `let [b[let]] = []`), identifiers inside
the index or template span were declared as lexical bindings, and in
destructuring assignment patterns (`[obj[{a = 42}].f()] = []`),
expression errors inside the index or template span were cleared by
`ValidateAsPattern()`.

Use `ParseExpression()` (which sets up both `ExpressionParsingScope` and
`AcceptINScope`) instead.

Bug: 567222737
TAG=agy
CONV=905f9f91-322f-467a-9f81-1f78a365b0d8

